the loop statement is used to process a  group of SAS statements multiple times  using loops can greatly reduce the  number of statements required for  repetitive calculations or reading huge  data sets the syntax for using do loop  is as shown here the index variable  stores the value of the current  iteration of do loop an already created  variable is eligible to become the index  for do loop the start value specifies  the initial value of the index variable  the stop value is the last index value  that executes the do loop the optional  by clause specifies an increment value  for the index variable if by Clause is  not specified the default increment  value is 1 it is important to note that  the start stop and increment values are  set upon entry into the do loop they  cannot be changed during the processing  of the do loop they can be numbers  variables or SAS expressions the end  statement terminates the loop the value  of the index variable can be changed  within the loop in the next screen we  will observe an example of do loop  the gampel will keep the amount of  interest earned each month for a  one-year investment by a person during  compilation the program data vector is  created for the finance dot earnings  data set when the data step is executed  the values of a mount and rate are  assigned the do loop is executed during  each execution of the do loop the value  of earned is calculated and is added to  its previous value then the value of  month is incremented on the 12th  execution of the do loop the program  data vector looks as shown after the  12th execution of the do loop the value  of month is incremented to 13 because 13  exceeds the stop value of the iterative  do statement the do loop stops executing  and processing continues to the next  data step statement the end of the data  step is reached the values are written  to the finance dot earnings data set and  in this example the data step ends only  one observation is written to the data  set however there might be a situation  when the user wants to store earning for  each month individually in the resulting  data set in such a case explicit output  statements are used with the do loop we  will discuss this in the next screen  fault data step contains an implicit  output statement at the end of the step  but placing an explicit output statement  overrides automatic output causing SAS  to add an observation to the data set  every time the explicit output statement  is executed for example if we use the  explicit output statement in the do loop  as shown in the program calculations for  each value of the index variable will be  stored separately in the output data set  the output will appear as shown so far  we discussed how a do loop iterates with  an increment in the index value in each  iteration however you can control the  flow of loop and some other methods too  we will discuss these methods in the  next screen
 

